# How Model Training Actually Works

A ground-up explanation of what happens between "I have a folder of labelled images"
and "I have a model that detects backpacks under bushes." Written against your
specific pipeline (YOLO-family detectors, HERIDAL/Weitefeld/bush data, tiling,
occlusion sweeps) so the abstract concepts have concrete anchors.

---

## 1. The big picture, in one paragraph

A detector is a function that takes an image and outputs a list of boxes plus class
labels plus confidence scores. That function has millions of internal numbers
("weights") that determine what it does. Training is the process of nudging those
numbers, repeatedly, so the function's outputs get closer to what your labelled
data says they should be. You never write the rules "a backpack has straps and a
rectangular body" by hand — you show the model thousands of labelled examples and
let an optimisation algorithm discover numbers that happen to produce correct
outputs. Everything below is the machinery around that one idea.

---

## 2. Data splits: train, validation, test

You never train and evaluate on the same images. Here's why, and what each split is
actually for.

### Train split

The images the model is *actually updated on*. Every weight change happens because
of an image in this set. This is typically 70-80% of your data.

### Validation split

Images the model **never trains on**, but that you check performance on *during*
training — usually after every epoch (one full pass through the training data). This
answers: "is the model actually learning something general, or has it started
memorising the training images?"

This matters because of **overfitting**. A model with millions of weights has more
than enough capacity to simply memorise your training set — get 100% accuracy on
images it's seen, and 0% on anything new. Validation performance is your early
warning system: if training accuracy keeps climbing but validation accuracy plateaus
or drops, the model has started memorising rather than generalising, and you stop
training (this is called **early stopping**).

Validation is also what you use to make *decisions* during development — which
learning rate worked better, which augmentation helped, when to stop. Because you
make decisions based on it, it's technically "used" in a soft way, even though no
weight update ever sees it directly.

### Test split

Images the model never trains on **and that you never look at until the very end**.
No decisions get made based on test performance — no hyperparameter tuning, no "let
me try a different learning rate and see if the test number improves." You run it
exactly once, report the number, and that's your thesis result.

Why the strict separation from validation? Because if you tune decisions against the
test set, you're indirectly fitting to it — just more slowly than direct training.
The number you report would then overstate how well the model will do on genuinely
new data. The test set is your one honest simulation of "data the model has truly
never influenced in any way."

### Why splitting is harder than "randomly pick 20%"

This is the part that trips people up, and it's the reason `heridal_to_yolo.py` and
`weitefeld_to_yolo.py` both do something more careful than a random per-image split.

If your images have **near-duplicates** — overlapping tiles from the same photo,
multiple stills of the same physical object from slightly different angles, frames
from one video clip — and you split randomly, near-identical images can land on both
sides of the split. The model then "tests" on something it has effectively already
seen a near-copy of during training. Your test accuracy looks great and is a lie.

This is why:
- **HERIDAL tiles are split by source photo**, not by tile — all 20 tiles from one
  photo go entirely into train, or entirely into val, or entirely into test.
- **Weitefeld is split by physical finding**, not by image — since one tarp might be
  photographed 85 times from different angles, all 85 photos of that tarp go into
  one split, never spread across two.
- **Your own bush dataset is split by placement/session/site** — a burst of stills
  around one staged backpack all go into the same split.

The general rule: **find the true unit of independence in your data, and split on
that unit, not on the individual image.**

