## 3. Tiling: solving the small-object problem

Separate from splitting, tiling addresses a different issue: image resolution versus
object size.

A detector like YOLO resizes whatever image you give it down to a fixed input size
— commonly 640×640 or 1024×1024 pixels — before processing it, because the network's
internal computations are built for a fixed input shape.

Your source images are much bigger. HERIDAL photos are 4000×3000. If you feed a
4000×3000 photo straight into a detector expecting 1024×1024, it gets squashed down
by roughly 4×. A person who was 120 pixels tall in the original photo becomes about
30 pixels tall in what the network actually sees. Push that further — Weitefeld is
8416×6032, an 8× squash — and small objects can shrink to a handful of pixels,
below the point where there's enough visual information left to recognise them as
anything at all.

**Tiling fixes this by cutting the big photo into a grid of smaller crops first**,
each already close to the network's native input size, and running detection on
each crop separately. A person who was 30 pixels tall in the full squashed photo
might be 120 pixels tall within their own tile, because that tile only had to shrink
a small region rather than the whole scene.

Two details that matter in the implementation:

**Overlap between tiles.** If tiles didn't overlap, an object sitting exactly on the
boundary between two tiles would get cut in half in both of them — visible, but
incomplete, in either. `heridal_to_yolo.py` uses 20% overlap between neighbouring
tiles specifically so that most objects land fully inside at least one tile.

**What to do with objects that are still partially cut off.** Even with overlap,
some boxes land right at a tile edge and get partially clipped. The script has a
`--min-visible` threshold: if less than 30% of an object's original area survives
the clip, that whole tile gets **dropped** rather than kept with a tiny sliver box
or with the object silently unlabelled. Both alternatives (keeping a useless sliver
box, or keeping the crop but forgetting to label the object) would teach the model
something false, so dropping the ambiguous tile is the safer choice.

Tiling is why your dataset conversion scripts produce far more images than you
started with — one 4000×3000 HERIDAL photo becomes roughly 20 separate 1024×1024
tiles.

