## 4. What's actually inside the model: backbone, neck, head

A modern detector (YOLO and similar) is built in three conceptual stages, stacked
on top of each other. Understanding this is what makes the A0/A2/A2b comparison in
your Phase 3 plan make sense.

### Backbone

The backbone is a stack of convolutional layers that takes the raw image and
progressively extracts **features** — patterns at increasing levels of abstraction.
Early layers detect simple things like edges and colour gradients. Middle layers
combine those into textures and shapes. Later layers combine those into
object-part-level concepts. The backbone doesn't know what a "backpack" is; it just
produces a rich numerical description of what's visually present in the image at
several different scales.

This is the most computationally expensive part of the network, and it's also the
most *transferable* — a backbone trained on any large image dataset (like COCO)
already knows general things like "edges," "texture," and "object-like blobs" that
are useful regardless of what specific objects you eventually want to detect.

### Neck

A smaller set of layers that combines the backbone's features from different scales
— because a distant small backpack and a close-up large one produce useful signal at
different resolutions inside the backbone, and the neck merges that multi-scale
information so the network can detect objects of different sizes with one unified
representation. Feature Pyramid Networks (FPN) and PANet, which appear throughout
the YOLO literature, are neck designs.

### Head

The final layers that take the neck's merged features and actually produce the
output: for each region of the image, "is there an object here, what class is it,
and what are the box coordinates." This is the part that's directly tied to *your*
specific classes — the head has one output slot per class you're training it to
recognise.

### Why this three-part structure matters for your A0/A2 comparison

- **A0** — one backbone, one neck, **one head** that predicts all classes (person
  and every HPI category) together from the same output layer.
- **A2** — the same shared backbone and neck, but **two separate heads** branching
  off at the end — one specialised for `person`, one for the HPI classes. Because the
  expensive part (the backbone) is still shared and only run once, the second head
  adds relatively little extra computation — it's a small parallel branch, not a
  second full pass through the network. The benefit is that each head's training
  signal can be weighted and balanced independently, so the numerous `person`
  examples don't drown out learning signal for the much rarer HPI classes.
- **A2b** — two entirely separate models, each with its own full backbone, neck and
  head. Maximum separation, but you now pay for two full backbones at inference
  time, roughly doubling compute and memory — which is why it's the "stretch, only
  if time and evidence justify it" option in your plan.

