## 5. Weights, and what "pretrained" means

**Weights** are the actual numbers inside the network — millions of them, mostly
inside the convolutional filters of the backbone — that get adjusted during
training. Before training, they have to start at *some* value. Two options:

**Random initialisation.** Weights start as small random numbers. The network knows
nothing. It has to learn everything — including basic concepts like "edges exist" —
purely from your training data. This requires a lot of data to work well, since
there's nothing to build on.

**Pretrained weights.** Weights start at values learned from training on a large,
unrelated dataset first — almost always COCO (Common Objects in Context, ~330,000
everyday photos with 80 object categories: people, cars, dogs, chairs, and so on).
A backbone trained on COCO has already learned general-purpose visual features —
edges, textures, common object shapes — even though none of COCO's 80 classes are
"backpack in bush occluded by foliage." Those general features transfer usefully to
almost any new visual task.

**Fine-tuning** is what you do next: take those COCO-pretrained weights and continue
training on your own dataset, so the general features get specialised toward your
actual classes and your actual visual domain (aerial, bush, occlusion). This is
almost always dramatically more data-efficient than starting from random weights,
which is why `yolo11s.pt`, `yolo26s.pt` etc. in your training commands are files
containing COCO-pretrained weights — you download them once and every training run
starts from that point rather than from scratch.

This is also the substance of your D7 decision: COCO-pretrained is the default
starting point for every Phase 3 arm, with one side experiment testing whether
starting from Weitefeld-pretrained weights (already somewhat specialised toward
aerial forest occlusion) beats generic COCO as a starting point before your own
bush-specific fine-tuning happens.

**Training from scratch isn't defensible for any arm in your current sweep.** All six
candidates assume a pretrained checkpoint, and your dataset — roughly 40 placements
per class — is far too small to learn general visual features from nothing. COCO
initialisation is the default everywhere, with the Weitefeld-init side experiment as
the only variation.

