## 8. Evaluation: how "good" gets measured

### IoU (Intersection over Union)

The basic building block for scoring a single predicted box against a single true
box. It's the overlapping area between the predicted box and the ground-truth box,
divided by the total area covered by either one combined. A perfect match scores
1.0; no overlap at all scores 0.0. A prediction only "counts" as correct if its IoU
against the true box clears some threshold — commonly 0.5.

### Precision and recall

For a given confidence threshold (how sure the model has to be before it reports a
detection):

- **Precision** — of everything the model claimed was a detection, what fraction
  were actually correct? Low precision means lots of false alarms.
- **Recall** — of everything that was actually there, what fraction did the model
  find? Low recall means it's missing real targets.

These trade off against each other: lowering the confidence threshold catches more
real objects (higher recall) but also lets through more false alarms (lower
precision). For a SAR application the acceptable trade-off point is a real decision,
not an automatic one — missing a real person is worse than a false alarm that a
human operator has to dismiss.

### mAP (mean Average Precision)

The standard single-number summary metric. For each class, you compute the
precision-recall curve (precision at every possible recall level as the confidence
threshold slides), find the area under that curve (Average Precision, AP), then
average AP across all classes to get mAP.

**mAP@50** means AP was computed using an IoU threshold of 0.5 to decide whether a
box "counts." **mAP@50-95** is stricter — it averages AP across IoU thresholds from
0.5 to 0.95 in steps, rewarding models whose boxes are not just roughly right but
tightly, precisely right.

### Why per-class matters more than aggregate, in your case specifically

mAP as usually reported is an *average across all classes*. If your dataset has far
more `person` examples than any single HPI class (very likely, given how much
easier person data is to collect), a model can score a respectable aggregate mAP
almost entirely on the strength of doing well on `person`, while quietly scoring
near zero on your rarest HPI classes — and the aggregate number would hide that
completely. This is exactly why your `runs.csv` schema logs one row **per class per
run** rather than one row per run: the aggregate number is close to meaningless for
answering your actual research question, which is specifically about the rare
classes.

