## 9. Where this all lands in your pipeline

Putting the whole chapter together against your actual project:

1. **Split** your source data (HERIDAL, Weitefeld, your own bush stills) into
   train/val/test, on the correct unit of independence (source photo, physical
   finding, or placement — never the raw image/tile).
2. **Tile** the oversized source images so small objects don't disappear when
   resized to the network's input size.
3. **Load a backbone** starting from COCO-pretrained weights (or, in the D7 side
   experiment, Weitefeld-pretrained weights) rather than random initialisation.
4. **Choose a head configuration** — A0's single multi-class head, or A2's
   shared-backbone dual-head split — depending on which stage of Phase 3 you're in.
5. **Train**: repeated batches of forward pass → loss → backprop → optimiser update,
   for the specified number of epochs, with augmentation applied to every image
   shown, and validation checked periodically to catch overfitting and pick the best
   checkpoint.
6. **Evaluate on the untouched test split**, computing precision/recall/mAP@50
   per class, and separately on the frozen occlusion buckets to produce your
   degradation curves.
7. **Only then**, and only once, look at the true held-out numbers you report in
   the thesis.

Every script you've had built so far exists to get steps 1 and 2 right, because
getting those wrong silently corrupts every result that comes after — a leaked
split or a bad tiling job doesn't crash anything, it just quietly produces numbers
that look good and mean nothing.